If the atom-pair at depth n and bit-source i is not junk, and for a bit b the direct-refinement component of the generalized X-step is nonempty and its split component meets the basic neighbourhood of n, then the Y half-step atom at the code of b is not junk.

Code-state correctness rewrites the generated sides as basic neighbourhoods. If the bit is true, the nonempty refinement is a nonempty intersection, so its emptiness decision is 0, because the decision is at most 1 and the value 1 would make the intersection empty. If the bit is false, the same bound applies to the difference emptiness decision. The X sub-step on the packed atom-pair state therefore has junk flag 0, and its correctness identifies the second output index with the split component. That component meets the basic neighbourhood of n, so the intersection emptiness decision on the second presentation is 0 by the same bound. Unfolding the half-step junk flag, selection at the forced bit 1 and at the vanishing inner junk flag leaves that decision.
